**<u>Upper bound  $P\le 3$</u>**

If a monochromatic  $k\times k$  block has  $k\ge 4$ , then in each of its  $(k)$  rows there are at least  $(k)$  cells of that color. Since each row has  $7$  cells,  $k\ge4\Rightarrow k>7-k$ , so each of those rows is majority in that color. The same holds for the  $k$  columns.

* A blue  $k\times k$  block with  $k\ge4$  would force at least  $k$  blue majority columns; but the condition gives exactly  $4$  red majority columns, hence only  $3$  blue majority columns. Contradiction.

* A red  $k\times k$  block with  $k\ge4$  would force at least  $k$  red majority rows; but there are exactly  $4$  blue majority rows, hence only  $3$  red majority rows. Contradiction.

Thus no monochromatic square can have side length  $4$  or more, so  $P\le3$ .**<u>Construction with  $P=3$</u>**

We will color to get a  $3\times3$  red square and meet the row/column counts.

